# Claim 1

**1.** A computer-implemented method for blockchain-based access control, the method comprising:

**(a)** receiving, at a node of a distributed blockchain network, a plurality of records, each record of the plurality of records being digitally signed with a cryptographic signature associated with an originating entity;

**(b)** validating, by one or more miner nodes of the distributed blockchain network, each digitally signed record of the plurality of records by:
   - verifying the cryptographic signature against a public key associated with the originating entity, and
   - confirming that the record satisfies a consensus condition of the blockchain network;

**(c)** appending, responsive to successful validation, each validated record to a block of a distributed ledger maintained across the distributed blockchain network;

**(d)** storing, on the distributed ledger, at least one access control role definition, the access control role definition comprising:
   - a role identifier, and
   - a permission specification defining one or more permitted operations that a holder of the role is authorized to perform with respect to one or more of the digitally signed records;

**(e)** receiving an access request from a requesting entity to perform a requested operation on a target record of the plurality of digitally signed records;

**(f)** determining, by reference to the access control role definition stored on the distributed ledger, whether the requesting entity is associated with a role having a permission specification that authorizes the requested operation on the target record;

**(g)** granting the requesting entity access to perform the requested operation only when the permission specification authorizes the requested operation; and

**(h)** executing, responsive to the granting of access, a cryptocurrency transfer transaction that transfers a cryptocurrency amount to a consumer record maintained on the distributed ledger, the cryptocurrency transfer transaction being validated by the one or more miner nodes and recorded in a subsequent block of the distributed ledger.

